rd what in the cilamar meeting  help  type meeting help to bring together  these two opposing views and try to  develop some kind of consensus right and  the answer that we found was they were  almost irreconcilable views and the  views on the opposition were mostly  cultural  they weren't science they weren't  questioning the scientific thing in  large part in large in the maine in  europe particularly it was the desire to  try to maintain the traditional type of  agriculture so we just never could get  the two sides  even on safety issues because i know  even the opponents often claim that  their safety problems right so the  companies set up trials they did all  kinds of the fda approved many of these  things after  examining the data  there was always a problem of  dissemination of weeds  weeds that would take over all of these  kinds of things arose  and  slowly but surely the scientific data  dispelled almost all of them  but never could challenge  people at the core values of their  culture  they felt about food what food meant to  them right and in many ways one of the  problems i think early on  and the ag business recognized it is  all the genetic modification they were  making were to facilitate better yields  for the farmers right make work easier  for the farmers  the public would say well what do i get  out of it i'm taking the risk of eating  something that you tell me is safe but  may turn out not to be safe in the long  run so why should i take that risk when  all it's doing is making a farmer's job  easier and  richer so is this a case maybe of the  scientific community not finding the  right way to explain or the right  message or the right well i'm not sure  what kind of message you could do  because it's we'll get to it  you could tell people that it would make  food less expensive for them or more  neutral all of those arguments were made  and they didn't they didn't work no and  i  my own view thought  ultimately the shopper is going to walk  